</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Reward Model Design</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Naive reward models typically use a simple linear layer as the reward head. We find that using an MLP for the reward head significantly improves the RM’s capability. Two main elements contribute to this:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S4.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S4.I3.i1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\diamond" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋄</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\diamond</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Layer Number</span>: The number of layers in the reward model head determines the model’s capacity and learning capability. An excessive number of layers can lead to increased training complexity, while too few layers may restrict the model’s representational power.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S4.I3.i2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\diamond" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋄</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\diamond</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Choice of Activation Function</span>: The activation function is crucial for model training. Different activation functions, such as ReLU or Tanh, affect the model’s non-linear mapping ability and the gradient flow during the training process.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.1 Reward Modeling Approaches ‣ Experimental Analysis ‣ BaseReward: A Strong Baseline for Multimodal Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we summarize the following experimental findings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S4.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S4.I4.i1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\diamond" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋄</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\diamond</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Both the number of layers in the reward head and the choice of activation function have a significant impact on the final performance of the reward modeling. Using only a 1-layer linear head yields the worst results.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S4.I4.i2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\diamond" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋄</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\diamond</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The best reward modeling performance is achieved when the number of layers is 2 and the SiLU activation function is used. Other activation functions, as well as more layers, do not bring significant performance gains.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In subsequent experiments, we default to using a configuration with 2 layers and SiLU activation function.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Training Regularization Strategies</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">During the training process of the reward model, we conduct a detailed ablation study on two common regularization strategies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S4.I5" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S4.I5.i1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\diamond" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋄</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\diamond</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S4.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I5.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Zero-Coefficient Regularization.</span> This technique applies a penalty to encourage the rewards for both chosen (<math id="S4.I5.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>) and rejected (<math id="S4.I5.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>) responses to be centered around zero. The regularization term is formulated as the mean of the squared sum of the rewards.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S4.I5.i2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\diamond" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋄</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\diamond</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S4.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I5.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Length Normalization.</span> This strategy aims to mitigate the reward model’s intrinsic bias towards longer responses. It normalizes the predicted reward by the logarithm of the response length.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The core ranking loss, which is a function of the reward model’s parameters <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, is formally defined as:</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{Reward}}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{x,y_{w},y_{l}}\left[-\log\sigma\left(r(y_{w}|x)-r(y_{l}|x)\right)\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>Reward</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{Reward}}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{x,y_{w},y_{l}}\left[-\log\sigma\left(r(y_{w}|x)-r(y_{l}|x)\right)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is the sigmoid function, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is the prompt, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> is the preferred (winner) response, and <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is the rejected (loser) response. The regularization techniques modify these rewards or the overall loss function as described in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4.3 Training Regularization Strategies ‣ Experimental Analysis ‣ BaseReward: A Strong Baseline for Multimodal Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 4.3 Training Regularization Strategies ‣ Experimental Analysis ‣ BaseReward: A Strong Baseline for Multimodal Reward Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we adjust the weight of the zero-coefficient regularizer, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, from 0 to 0.1. The results indicate a discernible performance degradation across various metrics as <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> increases. Furthermore, the inclusion of length normalization alone (represented by the dashed line) does not yield any performance improvement compared to the baseline without regularization (the point where <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math>). Consequently, we do not apply any regularization loss in the default configuration for training our reward model.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Regularization Strategies for Reward Model Training</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> winner rewards <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(y_{w}|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(y_{w}|x)</annotation></semantics></math>, loser rewards <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(y_{l}|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(y_{l}|x)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> winner lengths <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>, loser lengths <math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> regularization weight <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4:</span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">procedure</span> <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Length Normalization</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(y_{w}|x)\leftarrow r(y_{w}|x)/\log(l_{w}+1.0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(y_{w}|x)\leftarrow r(y_{w}|x)/\log(l_{w}+1.0)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(y_{l}|x)\leftarrow r(y_{l}|x)/\log(l_{l}+1.0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(y_{l}|x)\leftarrow r(y_{l}|x)/\log(l_{l}+1.0)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">7:</span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">procedure</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">8:</span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">procedure</span> <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Loss Computation</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">9:</span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{Reward}}\leftarrow-\text{mean}(\text{logsigmoid}(r(y_{w}|x)-r(y_{l}|x)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>Reward</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mtext>mean</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>logsigmoid</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{Reward}}\leftarrow-\text{mean}(\text{logsigmoid}(r(y_{w}|x)-r(y_{l}|x)))</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">10:</span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{zero-coeff}}\leftarrow\lambda\times\text{mean}((r(y_{w}|x)+r(y_{l}|x))^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>zero-coeff</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>mean</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{zero-coeff}}\leftarrow\lambda\times\text{mean}((r(y_{w}|x)+r(y_{l}|x))^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">11:</span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}}\leftarrow\mathcal{L}_{\text{Reward}}+\mathcal{L}_{\text{zero-coeff}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>Reward</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>zero-coeff</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}}\leftarrow\mathcal{L}_{\text{Reward}}+\mathcal{L}_{\text{zero-coeff}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">12:</span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg1.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">13:</span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">procedure</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.16127v1/lambda_metrics_line_chart.svg" id="S4.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/141;" width="476" height="141"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><span id="S4.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The Effect of Different Regularization Strategies on Reward Model Performance.</span> The solid red line shows the performance variation with <math id="S4.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. The dashed line represents a baseline model trained with only length normalization and no zero-coefficient regularization (<math id="S4.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math>). The results show that performance generally declines as <math id="S4.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> increases from zero.</figcaption>
</figure>
